%% chapter-24.ltx — Small-Step (Structural) Operational Semantics
%% Part III: Formal Semantics

%% ============================================================
%% 24.1 Introduction
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:small-step-intro}

Small-step semantics (also called \emph{structural operational semantics},
or SOS) describes computation as a sequence of individual steps.  Each step
transforms a program configuration into a slightly simpler one, until a
final value (or error) is reached.  Unlike big-step semantics, which jumps
directly from input to output, small-step semantics reveals the internal
structure of computation.

This chapter develops small-step semantics in depth.  We begin with
transition systems and the basic concepts of configurations and reduction
rules.  We then develop evaluation contexts and congruence rules as
techniques for structuring small-step semantics.  We prove the equivalence
of small-step and big-step semantics for several languages.  We illustrate
the approach with examples: the while language, the lambda calculus, and an
imperative language with pointers.  We conclude by examining the advantages
of small-step semantics: its ability to handle concurrency,
nontermination, and interleaving.

\begin{definition}[Small-Step Semantics]\label{def:small-step}
  \emph{Small-step semantics} defines computation as a transition system
  $(\texttt{Config}, \stepsto)$ where $\texttt{Config}$ is a set of
  configurations and $\stepsto \subseteq \texttt{Config} \times
  \texttt{Config}$ is a one-step reduction relation.  Computation is the
  transitive reflexive closure $\multistepsto$ of $\stepsto$.
\end{definition}

\begin{historical}
Small-step semantics was introduced by Gordon Plotkin in his seminal 1981
paper ``A Structural Approach to Operational Semantics''~\cite{plotkin1981structural}.
The key innovation was the use of \emph{evaluation contexts} (called
``evaluation structures'' in Plotkin's original paper) to specify where
reductions can occur.  This approach has become the standard for formal
semantics in the programming language research community.
\end{historical}

%% ============================================================
%% 24.2 Transition Systems
%% ============================================================
\section{Transition Systems}\label{sec:transition-systems}

\subsection{Basic Definitions}\label{sec:transition-basic}

\begin{definition}[Transition System]\label{def:transition-system}
  A \emph{transition system} is a triple $(C, \longrightarrow, \gamma_0)$
  where:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item $C$ is a set of \emph{configurations}.
    \item $\longrightarrow \;\subseteq C \times C$ is a \emph{transition
      relation} (one-step reduction).
    \item $\gamma_0 \in C$ is the \emph{initial configuration}.
  \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Multi-Step Reduction]\label{def:multi-step}
  The \emph{multi-step reduction} relation $\multistepsto$ is the
  reflexive-transitive closure of $\stepsto$:
  \begin{align*}
    \gamma &\multistepsto \gamma \quad \text{(reflexivity)} \\
    \gamma \stepsto \gamma' \quad &\Rightarrow \quad \gamma \multistepsto \gamma' \quad \text{(one step)} \\
    \gamma \multistepsto \gamma' \quad \gamma' \multistepsto \gamma'' \quad &\Rightarrow \quad \gamma \multistepsto \gamma'' \quad \text{(transitivity)}
  \end{align*}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Normal Form]\label{def:normal-form}
  A configuration $\gamma$ is in \emph{normal form} if there is no
  $\gamma'$ such that $\gamma \stepsto \gamma'$.  Normal forms are the
  ``answers'' of computation.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Kinds of Normal Forms}\label{sec:normal-forms}

Normal forms can be classified:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Values}: Normal forms that represent completed computations
    (e.g., integer literals, closures).
  \item \textbf{Errors}: Normal forms that represent runtime errors (e.g.,
    type errors, division by zero).
  \item \textbf{Stuck}: Normal forms that are neither values nor errors.
    These indicate a flaw in the semantics or the type system.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Stuck Configuration]\label{def:stuck-config}
  A configuration $\gamma$ is \emph{stuck} if it is in normal form but is
  not a value and not an error.  A type-safe language has no stuck
  configurations for well-typed programs.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Divergence}\label{sec:divergence}

\begin{definition}[Divergence]\label{def:divergence-small}
  A configuration $\gamma$ \emph{diverges} (written $\gamma \Uparrow$) if
  there exists an infinite reduction sequence:
  \[
    \gamma \stepsto \gamma_1 \stepsto \gamma_2 \stepsto \ldots
  \]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
  For a deterministic transition system, every configuration either:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Reaches a normal form (terminates), or
    \item Diverges.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

%% ============================================================
%% 24.3 Configurations and Reduction Rules
%% ============================================================
\section{Configurations and Reduction Rules}\label{sec:configurations-rules}

\subsection{Configuration Structure}\label{sec:config-structure}

The structure of configurations depends on the language:

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Configuration Structures}
  \label{tab:config-structures}
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Language Feature} & \textbf{Configuration} \\
    \hline
    Pure expressions & $e$ \\
    With environment & $\bconfig{e, \rho}$ \\
    With store & $\config{e}{\rho}{\mu}$ \\
    With continuation & $\config{e}{\rho}{\mu}{\kappa}$ \\
    Concurrent & $\config{e_1 \| e_2}{\rho}{\mu}$ \\
    With exceptions & $\bconfig{e, \sigma} \mid \texttt{raise}\ v$ \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Reduction Rules}\label{sec:reduction-rules}

Reduction rules define the one-step transition relation.  They come in
several forms:

\subsubsection{Computational Rules (Computation)}

These define the ``real work'' of computation:

\begin{semrules}{Beta Reduction}
  \semruleaxiom{
    (\lambda x.\, e)\, v \stepsto e[v/x]
  }{\text{($\beta$)}}
\end{semrules}

\subsubsection{Structural Rules (Congruence)}

These define where computation can occur:

\begin{semrules}{Congruence for Application}
  \semrule{
    e_1 \stepsto e_1'
  }{
    e_1\ e_2 \stepsto e_1'\ e_2
  }{\text{(App-L)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    e_2 \stepsto e_2'
  }{
    v\ e_2 \stepsto v\ e_2'
  }{\text{(App-R)}}
\end{semrules}

\subsubsection{Error Rules}

These define error conditions:

\begin{semrules}{Error Rules}
  \semruleaxiom{
    v_1 + v_2 \stepsto \texttt{error} \quad \text{if } v_1, v_2 \text{ not both integers}
  }{\text{(Add-Err)}} \\[6pt]
  \semruleaxiom{
    v_1\ v_2 \stepsto \texttt{error} \quad \text{if } v_1 \text{ not a closure}
  }{\text{(App-Err)}}
\end{semrules}

%% ============================================================
%% 24.4 Evaluation Contexts
%% ============================================================
\section{Evaluation Contexts}\label{sec:evaluation-contexts}

\subsection{The Problem with Congruence Rules}\label{sec:congruence-problem}

Congruence rules are tedious and error-prone.  For a language with many
constructs, the number of congruence rules grows quadratically.  Evaluation
contexts provide a more elegant solution.

\subsection{Evaluation Contexts Defined}\label{sec:eval-contexts-defined}

\begin{definition}[Evaluation Context]\label{def:eval-context}
  An \emph{evaluation context} $E$ is a term with a single ``hole''
  $\hole$ indicating where the next reduction step occurs.  Evaluation
  contexts are defined by a grammar that specifies the evaluation order.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Context Filling]\label{def:context-filling}
  The \emph{filling} of an evaluation context $E$ with a term $e$, written
  $E[e]$, is the result of replacing the hole $\hole$ in $E$ with $e$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Evaluation Contexts for Call-by-Value]\label{ex:eval-ctx-cbv}
  For a call-by-value lambda calculus:
  \begin{grammar}{Evaluation Contexts}
    \production{E}{\hole \alt E\ e \alt v\ E \alt E + e \alt v + E \alt \mathbf{if}\ E\ \mathbf{then}\ e_1\ \mathbf{else}\ e_2}
  \end{grammar}
  The hole can appear:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item At the top level.
    \item In the function position of an application (evaluate function first).
    \item In the argument position, but only if the function is a value.
    \item In the condition of an \texttt{if}.
  \end{itemize}
\end{example}

\subsection{The Evaluation Context Rule}\label{sec:eval-ctx-rule}

The key insight is that all structural rules can be replaced by a single
rule:

\begin{semrules}{Evaluation Context}
  \semrule{
    e \stepsto e'
  }{
    E[e] \stepsto E[e']
  }{\text{(Ctx)}}
\end{semrules}

This single rule replaces all congruence rules.  The evaluation order is
encoded in the grammar of evaluation contexts.

\begin{example}[Reduction with Evaluation Contexts]\label{ex:reduction-ctx}
  Consider $(\lambda x.\, x + 1)\, (2 + 3)$:
  \begin{align*}
    & (\lambda x.\, x + 1)\, (2 + 3) \\
    =\ & E[(2 + 3)] \quad \text{where } E = (\lambda x.\, x + 1)\, \hole \\
    \stepsto\ & E[5] \quad \text{by arithmetic} \\
    =\ & (\lambda x.\, x + 1)\, 5 \\
    =\ & E'[5] \quad \text{where } E' = \hole \\
    \stepsto\ & 5 + 1 \quad \text{by } \beta\text{-reduction} \\
    \stepsto\ & 6 \quad \text{by arithmetic}
  \end{align*}
\end{example}

\subsection{Evaluation Strategies}\label{sec:eval-strategies-ctx}

Different evaluation strategies correspond to different grammars of
evaluation contexts:

\subsubsection{Call-by-Value (CBV)}

\begin{grammar}{CBV Evaluation Contexts}
  \production{E}{\hole \alt E\ e \alt v\ E}
\end{grammar}

Evaluate the function, then the argument, then apply.

\subsubsection{Call-by-Name (CBN)}

\begin{grammar}{CBN Evaluation Contexts}
  \production{E}{\hole \alt E\ e}
\end{grammar}

Evaluate the function, then apply (without evaluating the argument).

\subsubsection{Call-by-Need (Lazy)}

Lazy evaluation uses a more complex context grammar that accounts for
sharing of thunks.

%% ============================================================
%% 24.5 Congruence Rules
%% ============================================================
\section{Congruence Rules}\label{sec:congruence-rules}

While evaluation contexts subsume congruence rules, it is instructive to
understand the traditional approach.

\subsection{Traditional Congruence Rules}\label{sec:traditional-congruence}

\begin{semrules}{Traditional Congruence}
  \semrule{
    e_1 \stepsto e_1'
  }{
    e_1 + e_2 \stepsto e_1' + e_2
  }{\text{(Add-L)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    e_2 \stepsto e_2'
  }{
    v + e_2 \stepsto v + e_2'
  }{\text{(Add-R)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    e_1 \stepsto e_1'
  }{
    e_1\ e_2 \stepsto e_1'\ e_2
  }{\text{(App-L)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    e_2 \stepsto e_2'
  }{
    v\ e_2 \stepsto v\ e_2'
  }{\text{(App-R)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    b \stepsto b'
  }{
    \mathbf{if}\ b\ \mathbf{then}\ e_1\ \mathbf{else}\ e_2 \stepsto \mathbf{if}\ b'\ \mathbf{then}\ e_1\ \mathbf{else}\ e_2
  }{\text{(If)}}
\end{semrules}

\subsection{Equivalence of Approaches}\label{sec:equivalence-approaches}

\begin{theorem}[Context vs. Congruence Equivalence]\label{thm:ctx-cong-equiv}
  The transition system defined by evaluation contexts is equivalent to the
  transition system defined by congruence rules: they produce the same
  transition relation.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
  By induction on the structure of evaluation contexts.  Each evaluation
  context rule corresponds to a sequence of congruence rules, and vice
  versa.
\end{proof}

%% ============================================================
%% 24.6 Proving Equivalence of Small-Step and Big-Step
%% ============================================================
\section{Proving Equivalence of Small-Step and Big-Step}\label{sec:equivalence-small-big}

\subsection{The Equivalence Theorem}\label{sec:equivalence-theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Small-Step/Big-Step Equivalence]\label{thm:small-big-equiv}
  For a deterministic language:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item If $\bconfig{e, \sigma} \evalto v$ (big-step), then
      $\bconfig{e, \sigma} \multistepsto v$ (small-step).
    \item If $\bconfig{e, \sigma} \multistepsto v$ and $v$ is a value, then
      $\bconfig{e, \sigma} \evalto v$.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Proof Strategy}\label{sec:proof-strategy-small}

The proof proceeds by mutual induction:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Big-step to small-step}: By induction on the big-step
    derivation.  For each rule, show that the small-step reduction sequence
    produces the same result.
  \item \textbf{Small-step to big-step}: By induction on the small-step
    reduction sequence.  Use a ``progress'' lemma to show that each step
    corresponds to a big-step derivation.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Example: IMP Language}\label{sec:imp-equivalence}

\begin{lemma}[IMP Big-Step to Small-Step]\label{lem:imp-big-to-small}
  If $\config{S, \sigma} \evalto \config{\mathbf{skip}, \sigma'}$ then
  $\config{S, \sigma} \multistepsto \config{\mathbf{skip}, \sigma'}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
  By induction on the big-step derivation.
  
  \textbf{Case} \textsc{Skip}: $\config{\mathbf{skip}, \sigma} \evalto
  \config{\mathbf{skip}, \sigma}$.  Zero small-step steps needed.
  
  \textbf{Case} \textsc{Seq}: $\config{S_1 ; S_2, \sigma} \evalto
  \config{\mathbf{skip}, \sigma''}$ via $\config{S_1, \sigma} \evalto
  \config{\mathbf{skip}, \sigma'}$ and $\config{S_2, \sigma'} \evalto
  \config{\mathbf{skip}, \sigma''}$.  By induction,
  $\config{S_1, \sigma} \multistepsto \config{\mathbf{skip}, \sigma'}$ and
  $\config{S_2, \sigma'} \multistepsto \config{\mathbf{skip}, \sigma''}$.
  The small-step rule for sequencing gives
  $\config{S_1 ; S_2, \sigma} \stepsto \config{S_1' ; S_2, \sigma'}$ for
  each step of $S_1$, followed by $\config{S_2, \sigma'} \multistepsto
  \config{\mathbf{skip}, \sigma''}$.
\end{proof}

%% ============================================================
%% 24.7 Examples
%% ============================================================
\section{Examples}\label{sec:small-step-examples}

\subsection{While Language}\label{sec:while-small-step}

\begin{semrules}{While Small-Step}
  \semruleaxiom{
    \config{\mathbf{skip} ; S, \sigma} \stepsto \config{S, \sigma}
  }{\text{(Seq-Skip)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    \config{S_1, \sigma} \stepsto \config{S_1', \sigma'}
  }{
    \config{S_1 ; S_2, \sigma} \stepsto \config{S_1' ; S_2, \sigma'}
  }{\text{(Seq-Step)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    \semB\sembrack{b}_\sigma = \mathbf{true}
  }{
    \config{\mathbf{if}\ b\ \mathbf{then}\ S_1\ \mathbf{else}\ S_2, \sigma} \stepsto \config{S_1, \sigma}
  }{\text{(If-T)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    \semB\sembrack{b}_\sigma = \mathbf{false}
  }{
    \config{\mathbf{if}\ b\ \mathbf{then}\ S_1\ \mathbf{else}\ S_2, \sigma} \stepsto \config{S_2, \sigma}
  }{\text{(If-F)}} \\[6pt]
  \semruleaxiom{
    \config{\mathbf{while}\ b\ \mathbf{do}\ S, \sigma} \stepsto \config{\mathbf{if}\ b\ \mathbf{then}\ (S ; \mathbf{while}\ b\ \mathbf{do}\ S)\ \mathbf{else}\ \mathbf{skip}, \sigma}
  }{\text{(While)}}
\end{semrules}

The \textsc{While} rule is remarkable: it unfolds the loop into an
\texttt{if} statement.  This allows nontermination to be expressed as an
infinite sequence of unfoldings.

\subsection{Lambda Calculus}\label{sec:lambda-small-step}

\begin{semrules}{Lambda Calculus Small-Step (CBV)}
  \semruleaxiom{
    (\lambda x.\, e)\, v \stepsto e[v/x]
  }{\text{($\beta$-CBV)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    e_1 \stepsto e_1'
  }{
    e_1\ e_2 \stepsto e_1'\ e_2
  }{\text{(App-L)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    e_2 \stepsto e_2'
  }{
    v\ e_2 \stepsto v\ e_2'
  }{\text{(App-R)}}
\end{semrules}

\begin{example}[Lambda Calculus Reduction]\label{ex:lambda-reduction}
  \begin{align*}
    & (\lambda x.\, \lambda y.\, x)\, 1\ 2 \\
    \stepsto\ & (\lambda y.\, 1)\ 2 \quad \text{by } \beta \\
    \stepsto\ & 1 \quad \text{by } \beta
  \end{align*}
\end{example}

\subsection{Imperative Language with Pointers}\label{sec:pointers-small-step}

\begin{semrules}{Pointer Operations}
  \semrule{
    l \notin \mathrm{dom}(\mu)
  }{
    \config{\mathbf{ref}\ v}{\rho}{\mu} \stepsto \config{l}{\rho}{\mu[l \mapsto v]}
  }{\text{(Ref)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    \mu(l) = v
  }{
    \config{\mathbf{!}l}{\rho}{\mu} \stepsto \config{v}{\rho}{\mu}
  }{\text{(Deref)}} \\[6pt]
  \semruleaxiom{
    \config{l \coloneqq v}{\rho}{\mu} \stepsto \config{()}{\rho}{\mu[l \mapsto v]}
  }{\text{(SetRef)}}
\end{semrules}

%% ============================================================
%% 24.8 Advantages: Concurrency, Nontermination, Interleaving
%% ============================================================
\section{Advantages: Concurrency, Nontermination, Interleaving}\label{sec:small-step-advantages}

\subsection{Nontermination}\label{sec:nontermination}

Small-step semantics can directly express nontermination:

\begin{example}[Infinite Loop]\label{ex:infinite-loop}
  The program $\mathbf{while}\ \mathbf{true}\ \mathbf{do}\ \mathbf{skip}$
  produces an infinite reduction sequence:
  \begin{align*}
    & \config{\mathbf{while}\ \mathbf{true}\ \mathbf{do}\ \mathbf{skip}, \sigma} \\
    \stepsto\ & \config{\mathbf{if}\ \mathbf{true}\ \mathbf{then}\ (\mathbf{skip} ; \mathbf{while}\ \mathbf{true}\ \mathbf{do}\ \mathbf{skip})\ \mathbf{else}\ \mathbf{skip}, \sigma} \\
    \stepsto\ & \config{\mathbf{skip} ; \mathbf{while}\ \mathbf{true}\ \mathbf{do}\ \mathbf{skip}, \sigma} \\
    \stepsto\ & \config{\mathbf{while}\ \mathbf{true}\ \mathbf{do}\ \mathbf{skip}, \sigma} \\
    \stepsto\ & \ldots
  \end{align*}
\end{example}

\subsection{Concurrency and Interleaving}\label{sec:concurrency-interleaving}

Small-step semantics can model concurrent execution through interleaving:

\begin{semrules}{Concurrent Execution}
  \semrule{
    \config{e_1, \sigma} \stepsto \config{e_1', \sigma'}
  }{
    \config{e_1 \| e_2, \sigma} \stepsto \config{e_1' \| e_2, \sigma'}
  }{\text{(Left)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    \config{e_2, \sigma} \stepsto \config{e_2', \sigma'}
  }{
    \config{e_1 \| e_2, \sigma} \stepsto \config{e_1 \| e_2', \sigma'}
  }{\text{(Right)}}
\end{semrules}

\begin{example}[Concurrent Execution]\label{ex:concurrent}
  Consider $x \coloneqq 1 \| x \coloneqq 2$:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item One interleaving: $x \coloneqq 1$ then $x \coloneqq 2$, final
      state $x = 2$.
    \item Another interleaving: $x \coloneqq 2$ then $x \coloneqq 1$,
      final state $x = 1$.
  \end{itemize}
  The transition system is non-deterministic: both outcomes are possible.
\end{example}

\subsection{Non-Determinism}\label{sec:non-determinism}

Small-step semantics naturally supports non-determinism:

\begin{definition}[Non-Deterministic Transition]\label{def:non-det}
  A transition system is \emph{non-deterministic} if there exists a
  configuration $\gamma$ such that $\gamma \stepsto \gamma_1$ and
  $\gamma \stepsto \gamma_2$ with $\gamma_1 \neq \gamma_2$.
\end{definition}

Non-determinism arises from:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Concurrent interleaving.
  \item Non-deterministic language constructs (e.g., \texttt{choose}).
  \item Abstract semantics (specifying all possible behaviors).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Exceptions and Control Effects}\label{sec:exceptions-small-step}

Small-step semantics handles exceptions naturally:

\begin{semrules}{Exceptions}
  \semruleaxiom{
    \config{\mathbf{raise}\ v, \sigma} \stepsto \config{\mathbf{raise}\ v, \sigma}
  }{\text{(Raise)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    e \stepsto e' \quad e' \neq \mathbf{raise}\ v
  }{
    \config{\mathbf{try}\ e\ \mathbf{catch}\ x \to h, \sigma} \stepsto \config{\mathbf{try}\ e'\ \mathbf{catch}\ x \to h, \sigma}
  }{\text{(Try-Step)}} \\[6pt]
  \semruleaxiom{
    \config{\mathbf{try}\ (\mathbf{raise}\ v)\ \mathbf{catch}\ x \to h, \sigma} \stepsto \config{h[v/x], \sigma}
  }{\text{(Try-Catch)}}
\end{semrules}

The exception ``propagates'' through the computation until it is caught by
a handler.

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Big-Step vs. Small-Step Comparison}
  \label{tab:big-vs-small}
  \begin{tabular}{lll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Property} & \textbf{Big-Step} & \textbf{Small-Step} \\
    \hline
    Termination & Can express & Can express \\
    Nontermination & Cannot express & Can express \\
    Concurrency & Cannot express & Can express \\
    Exceptions & Awkward & Natural \\
    Step counting & Cannot & Can \\
    Type soundness & Progress + preservation & Normal forms \\
    Proof complexity & Simpler & More complex \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

%% ============================================================
%% 24.9 Summary
%% ============================================================
\section{Summary}\label{sec:small-step-summary}

Small-step semantics provides a fine-grained view of computation as a
sequence of individual steps.  It reveals the internal structure of
evaluation, making it possible to reason about nontermination, concurrency,
exceptions, and other phenomena that big-step semantics cannot express.

The key technical innovation is the use of \emph{evaluation contexts} to
specify evaluation order.  This eliminates the need for numerous congruence
rules and provides a clean separation between computational rules (what
reduces) and structural rules (where reduction can occur).

Small-step semantics is the foundation for modern programming language
research.  It is used to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Define the semantics of concurrent and distributed languages.
  \item Prove type soundness (via progress and preservation).
  \item Reason about program equivalence and optimization.
  \item Model exceptions, continuations, and other control effects.
\end{itemize}

Together with big-step semantics, small-step semantics forms the core of
operational semantics---the most widely used approach to formal semantics
in programming language research.
